        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card card">
        <div class="feature-icon">
          <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <rect x="3" y="3" width="18" height="18" rx="2"/>
            <path d="M3 9h18"/>
            <path d="M9 21V9"/>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <h3 class="text-display-sm">Full HTTP request inspection</h3>
        <p class="text-body-md" style="color: var(--color-body);">
          View method, headers, query parameters, and body — beautifully formatted in a developer-first UI. Debug webhook payloads from any provider.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card card">
        <div class="feature-icon">
          <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
            <polyline points="12 6 12 12 16 14"/>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <h3 class="text-display-sm">7-day webhook sessions</h3>
        <p class="text-body-md" style="color: var(--color-body);">
          Each webhook endpoint stays active for 7 days and captures up to 500 requests with a live countdown timer.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card card">
        <div class="feature-icon">
          <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <rect x="3" y="11" width="18" height="11" rx="2" ry="2"/>
            <path d="M7 11V7a5 5 0 0 1 10 0v4"/>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <h3 class="text-display-sm">Private & secure</h3>
        <p class="text-body-md" style="color: var(--color-body);">
          Your webhook data is ephemeral and encrypted. Sessions auto-expire after 7 days. No data is ever sold or shared.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card card">
        <div class="feature-icon">
          <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <path d="M12 22s8-4 8-10V5l-8-3-8 3v7c0 6 8 10 8 10z"/>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <h3 class="text-display-sm">Works with every provider</h3>
        <p class="text-body-md" style="color: var(--color-body);">
          Test webhooks from Stripe, GitHub, Shopify, Discord, Slack, Twilio, PayPal, and any service that sends HTTP callbacks.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card card">
        <div class="feature-icon">
          <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <polyline points="16 18 22 12 16 6"/>
            <polyline points="8 6 2 12 8 18"/>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <h3 class="text-display-sm">Open source</h3>
        <p class="text-body-md" style="color: var(--color-body);">
          Fully open source on GitHub. Inspect the code, contribute features, or self-host your own webhook testing instance.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- What is a Webhook section — keyword-rich content for SEO -->
  <section class="content-section" id="what-is-a-webhook">
    <div class="content-container">
      <h2 class="text-display-md content-title">What is a Webhook?</h2>
      <div class="content-body">
        <p>A <strong>webhook</strong> (also called an HTTP callback or web callback) is an automated message sent from one application to another when a specific event occurs. Unlike traditional APIs where your application must repeatedly poll for new data, webhooks <strong>push data to your server in real-time</strong> the instant something happens.</p>
        
        <p>For example, when a customer completes a payment on <strong>Stripe</strong>, Stripe sends a webhook POST request to your server containing the payment details. When a developer pushes code to a <strong>GitHub</strong> repository, GitHub fires a webhook to your CI/CD pipeline. When a new order is placed on <strong>Shopify</strong>, Shopify sends a webhook with the order data.</p>

        <h3>How Webhooks Work</h3>
        <p>The webhook flow follows a simple pattern:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>You register a webhook URL</strong> — a publicly accessible HTTP endpoint on your server — with the webhook provider (Stripe, GitHub, Shopify, etc.).</li>
          <li><strong>An event occurs</strong> — such as a payment completing, a pull request being merged, or an order being placed.</li>
          <li><strong>The provider sends an HTTP POST request</strong> to your webhook URL with a JSON payload describing the event.</li>
          <li><strong>Your server processes the payload</strong> and returns an HTTP 200 response to acknowledge receipt.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3>Webhook vs API: What's the Difference?</h3>
        <p>The key difference between webhooks and APIs is the direction of communication. <strong>APIs are pull-based</strong> — your application sends a request to the server and waits for a response. <strong>Webhooks are push-based</strong> — the server sends data to your application without being asked. Webhooks are far more efficient for real-time event notifications because they eliminate unnecessary polling requests.</p>

        <h3>Why Use a Webhook Testing Tool?</h3>
        <p>During development, your local server (usually running on <code>localhost:3000</code>) isn't accessible from the public internet. Webhook providers like Stripe can't send HTTP requests to your local machine. A <strong>webhook testing tool</strong> like WebhookCatch solves this by providing a temporary, public URL that captures incoming requests and displays them in a visual dashboard — letting you inspect headers, body, query parameters, and HTTP method without deploying your code.</p>

        <div class="content-cta">
          <a href="/what-is-a-webhook" class="btn-link">Read the full webhook guide →</a>
          <a href="/webhook-vs-api" class="btn-link">Webhook vs API comparison →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Popular Integrations — targets long-tail searches -->
  <section class="integrations-section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2 class="text-display-md">Test webhooks from popular services</h2>
      <p class="text-body-md" style="color: var(--color-body);">WebhookCatch works with every service that sends HTTP callbacks. Here are the most popular integrations:</p>
    </div>
    <div class="integrations-grid">
      <a href="/stripe-webhook-tester" class="integration-card card">
        <h3>Stripe Webhooks</h3>
        <p>Test payment events, subscription updates, and invoice webhooks from Stripe.</p>
      </a>
      <a href="/github-webhook-tester" class="integration-card card">
