        <p>Test bot notifications, channel messages, and server event callbacks.</p>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- FAQ Section — targets "People also ask" -->
  <section class="faq-section" id="faq">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2 class="text-display-md">Frequently Asked Questions about Webhooks</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-list">
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary class="faq-question">What is a webhook?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>A webhook is an automated HTTP callback that sends real-time data from one application to another when a specific event occurs. Instead of polling an API repeatedly, webhooks push data to your server the instant something happens — like a payment completing on Stripe, a commit pushed to GitHub, or an order placed on Shopify.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary class="faq-question">How do I test a webhook?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>To test a webhook, use a tool like <a href="/">WebhookCatch.com</a> to generate a unique URL. Paste this URL into your webhook provider (Stripe, GitHub, Shopify, etc.) as the destination endpoint. When the provider fires an event, the request will appear in your WebhookCatch dashboard instantly — showing headers, body, method, and query parameters.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary class="faq-question">What is a webhook URL?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>A webhook URL is a publicly accessible HTTP endpoint on your server that receives incoming webhook payloads. When you configure a webhook in a service like Stripe or GitHub, you provide this URL so the service knows where to send event notifications. Tools like WebhookCatch generate temporary webhook URLs for testing purposes.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary class="faq-question">What is the difference between a webhook and an API?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>APIs use a pull model — your application sends a request to get data. Webhooks use a push model — the service sends data to your application automatically when an event occurs. APIs are client-initiated, while webhooks are server-initiated. Webhooks are more efficient for real-time notifications because they eliminate the need for constant polling.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary class="faq-question">How do I get a free webhook URL?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>Visit <a href="/">WebhookCatch.com</a> and click "Create Free Webhook URL". A unique, publicly accessible URL is generated instantly — no account or signup required. You can use this URL to receive and inspect webhook payloads from any service for free.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary class="faq-question">Are webhooks secure?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>Webhooks can be made secure by using HTTPS endpoints, verifying cryptographic signatures (HMAC), and checking timestamps to prevent replay attacks. Most major providers like Stripe, GitHub, and Shopify include HMAC signatures in webhook headers so you can verify the payload wasn't tampered with. <a href="/blog/webhook-security-hmac-signatures">Learn more about webhook security →</a></p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

